# SRG-APP-000171-AU-002540  -  CAT I
# For accounts using password authentication, the Central Log Server must be
# configured to store only cryptographic representations of passwords.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000171-AU-002540
group:           V-206474
severity:        high
cat:             CAT I
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-004062
control_rev5:    IA-5

contribution:    Splunk stores native account passwords only as a salted hash, and
                 passwordHashAlgorithm selects which hash is used for every password set or
                 changed from that point on.

governs:         authentication.conf - How users sign in, and the password rules applied to local accounts.

check:           authentication.conf [authentication] passwordHashAlgorithm matches ^SHA(256|512)-crypt(-[0-9]{4,})?$
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] passwordHashAlgorithm https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Passwords already stored keep the algorithm in force when they were set
                 (authentication.conf.spec:58-80), so re-hashing depends on a password
                 change procedure
external:        Credentials for accounts held in LDAP or SAML are stored by that provider,
                 not by Splunk
external:        File system protection of the Splunk password file and of splunk.secret,
                 provided by the operating system

citation:        SRG-APP-000171-AU-002540 check text; authentication.conf.spec:58-80
                 (passwordHashAlgorithm vocabulary SHA512-crypt, SHA256-crypt,
                 SHA512-crypt-<num_rounds>, SHA256-crypt-<num_rounds>, MD5-crypt; rounds
                 must be at least 1000; default SHA512-crypt; the setting controls how
                 hashed passwords are stored in the Splunk password file)
finding:         authentication.conf [authentication] passwordHashAlgorithm is set to
                 MD5-crypt or to a value outside the SHA256-crypt and SHA512-crypt
                 families, so the application does not store native account passwords as a
                 current cryptographic representation.
